    "created_at": "2016-08-23T17:50:07Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-08-23T17:50:07Z",
    "body": "> Shouldn't be here something like...\n> \n> ``` js\n> MyCustomElement.prototype = Object.create(HTMLElement.prototype);\n> MyCustomElement.prototype.constructor = MyCustomElement;\n> ```\n\nPlease go read https://esdiscuss.org/topic/extending-an-es6-class-using-es5-syntax, and ask related JS questions on http://stackoverflow.com.  This is an issue tracker for the **HTML specification**, not a support forum for every new feature in HTML / JS.\n\n> ``` js\n> class MyCustomElement extends HTMLElement {\n>   constructor() {\n>     super();  // can I omit this? Can browser call it somewhere else?\n>   }\n> }\n> ```\n\nYou can call `Reflect.construct(HTMLElement, [], MyCustomElement)` instead but you can't omit the call to `HTMLElement` constructor there since [HTML tree construction](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/syntax.html#tree-construction) cannot handle constructing a custom element resulting in either not a `HTMLElement` or a HTML element with a local name which is not equal to the given token.\n\n> What about if not set `extends` anywhere? What would be the default prototype for this CustomElement?\n\nYou could do:\n\n``` js\nclass MyCustomElement {\n    constructor() {\n        return Reflect.construct(HTMLElement, [], MyCustomElement);\n    }\n}\ncustomElements.define('my-custom-element', MyCustomElement);\n```\n\nAlthough this has a weird behavior that the constructed custom element would not have `HTMLElement` in its prototype chain whilst it's [branded](https://github.com/tc39/ecma262/issues/354) as `HTMLElement`.  The UA would continue to treat it as a `HTMLElement` but author scripts need to do something like `Element.prototype.getAttribute.call(element, \"class\")` to invoke `HTMLElement`'s method on the element.\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
